Polycystic Kidney Disease
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Palpable bilateral flank masses, Unexplained young-onset hypertension, Innumerable renal cysts on imaging

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-033
Reason for Visit: Sudden, severe exacerbation of unilateral flank pain and macroscopic haematuria, requiring urgent evaluation for a ruptured or infected renal cyst.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young adult with known polycystic kidney disease experiencing an acute, severe worsening of baseline flank pain accompanied by frankly bloody urine and a low-grade fever. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandated to evaluate for an acutely infected cyst or significant intra-cystic haemorrhage, conditions that can rapidly precipitate sepsis or acute kidney injury if left unassessed until regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My usual dull back ache suddenly became a sharp, tearing pain on the left side, and my urine looks like pure cranberry juice.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic, insidious onset of dull, persistent flank and lower back pain over several years. Tonight, they experienced a sudden, severe escalation of pain specifically in the left flank, indicating the likely rupture or hemorrhage of a large cyst. This was immediately followed by an episode of macroscopic haematuria, which often follows minor physical trauma, though none occurred today. They have a known history of new-onset, difficult-to-control severe hypertension diagnosed in their late twenties. They note generalized lethargy and a low-grade subjective fever, raising concern for an infected renal cyst. There is a strong family history of end-stage renal failure.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricted to lying on their right side to splint the severe left flank pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding the visible blood and fear of sudden total kidney failure.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 165/100 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.6C | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, diaphoretic, wincing when attempting to sit up straight or move their torso.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Deep palpation is limited by pain, but massive, firm, bilateral flank masses are grossly appreciable, consistent with their baseline disease. Back: Severe, exquisite costovertebral angle tenderness is specifically localized to the left side, sharply increased from baseline discomfort. Cardiovascular: Severe systemic hypertension is confirmed. Genitourinary: Point-of-care urinalysis reveals gross macroscopic haematuria and trace leukocytes, but no nitrites. Expected investigation results would include visualization of bilaterally massively enlarged kidneys entirely replaced by innumerable anechoic cysts on a targeted CT scan.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Compliant with prescribed ACE inhibitors, though blood pressure remains elevated due to the acute pain response.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Polycystic Kidney Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Suspected Cyst Rupture/Haemorrhage)
Differentials:

Acute Pyelonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary solitary cause due to the sudden, tearing nature of the pain and gross haematuria which strongly points to a mechanical cyst rupture rather than a purely infectious ascending process, though secondary infection is a risk.

Nephrolithiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of colicky, radiating groin pain; the pain is constant and localized to the massively enlarged renal capsule.

Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm Rupture - Ruled out/unlikely due to the patient's young age, stable hemodynamics (no profound shock), and the specific known history of structural kidney disease explaining the palpable masses.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This patient presents with an acute, severe complication of Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease, specifically a ruptured or haemorrhaging cyst, evidenced by the sudden, sharp localized pain and gross macroscopic haematuria. The mild fever and tachycardia suggest an accompanying inflammatory response. An urgent after-hours physical examination was strictly necessary to rule out life-threatening retroperitoneal bleeding, manage the severe acute pain, and determine if the patient required immediate hospital transfer for an infected, obstructed cyst. Delaying assessment would have exposed the patient to unacceptable risks of unmanaged severe pain and potential septic progression.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Paracetamol 1g and oral Oxycodone 5mg stat for immediate management of the severe, acute capsular stretch pain. Strictly instructed the patient to avoid all NSAIDs to prevent further renal impairment or exacerbation of bleeding. Ordered an urgent non-contrast CT KUB and formal urine microscopy and culture for the early morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: To continuously flush the urinary tract and prevent obstructing blood clots, the patient is instructed to consume 250mL of water every hour on the hour, setting a recurring alarm on their phone. The continuous visual feedback of the urine clearing from dark red to a lighter pink will strongly reinforce the habit of maintaining high-volume hydration during acute cyst bleeding episodes.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): None required.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 24 hours to review the CT scan, check for drops in haemoglobin, and evaluate renal function bloods.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 is required if the patient develops a sudden, explosive headache (risk of berry aneurysm rupture), feels faint or dizzy when standing, stops passing urine entirely, or develops high fevers with uncontrollable shivering.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The bleeding is highly likely from a burst cyst, but severe internal bleeding or obstruction must be formally ruled out by the morning CT scan.
Clinical Course: The sharp pain should dull into a heavy ache over the next 48 hours. The visible blood in the urine may persist intermittently for several days as the cyst cavity heals.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you suddenly experience the worst headache of your life, if you feel lightheaded and dizzy, or if you completely stop passing urine due to a clot, you must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: One of the many fluid-filled cysts on your kidney has likely burst or bled internally, stretching the sensitive outer casing of the kidney and causing the sudden, sharp pain and the blood in your urine. The strong pain relief will help immediately. We must avoid certain anti-inflammatories to protect your remaining kidney function.
Actionable Steps: Drink a large glass of water every hour to prevent blood clots from blocking your plumbing. Stop taking any ibuprofen or naproxen. Attend the urgent CT scan first thing tomorrow.

